#edb/ref 17592186147422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148273 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148273 #edb/ref 1061 196872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148273 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "07e18a18-e0f7-514d-9a1d-5e49ae4b0b94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148273 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148274 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148274 #edb/ref 1061 196875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148274 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "da8a83fe-da24-58c8-be84-aad5b70f01a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148274 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148275 #edb/ref 1061 196876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148275 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "20edaa81-4eeb-5d4b-98a7-837cb86e910d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148275 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148535 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148535 #edb/ref 1061 72289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148535 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e956a0b5-6283-59e2-b00b-6fff43cb6aaf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148535 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148536 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148536 #edb/ref 1061 72290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148536 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "20f09f61-d500-5013-be0d-df259b534912" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148536 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148537 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148537 #edb/ref 1061 72293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148537 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "58253af5-26fc-5261-b61b-97788f445428" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148537 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148538 #edb/ref 1061 72296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148538 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9f86a1a0-8db6-5c95-9948-5487d96d589a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148538 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148781 #edb/ref 1064 "To find a part given a percent and whole, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percent} \\times \\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part}\n$$\n\nHowever, we sometimes want to find a whole given a part and a percent. To do this, we can use the relationship between multiplication and division to rewrite the above formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{percent}\n$$\n\nLet's use this formula to find the missing number in the following statement:\n![](images/08/082c6ac53fa8de85b739dde0611943e92cb09d74d07378d6a33043d4b231e88d.svg)\n\nIn this case, the part is $540$, and the percent is $30\\%.$ \n\nWe can express $30\\%$ as a fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{30}{100} = \\dfrac{30\\div 10}{100\\div 10} = \\dfrac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nPutting these numbers into our formula for the whole, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =\\text{part}÷\\text{percent} \\\\  & =540÷30% \\\\  & =540÷\\frac{3}{10} \\\\  & =540\\times \\frac{10}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{540}{3}\\times 10 \\\\  & =180\\times 10 \\\\  & =1,800\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $1,800.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148781 #edb/ref 1065 8170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148781 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "69026176-778a-58ea-8ccf-185968679bd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148781 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148795 #edb/ref 1064 "We can use our knowledge of solving exponential equations to obtain information about a system that follows an exponential growth model.\n\nFor example, suppose that the revenue $R(t),$ in millions of dollars, of a rapidly growing startup company is given by the function\n\n$$\nR(t) = 2\\cdot 3^t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the number of years since the company was incorporated. How long will it take for the company's revenue to reach $\\[math]18$ million?\n\nTo find the solution to this problem, we need to solve the equation $R(t) = 18.$ This gives us the equation\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 3^t = 18.\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we first divide both sides by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {3}^{t} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot {3}^{t}}{2} & =\\frac{18}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot {3}^{t}}{\\cancel{2}} & =9 \\\\ {3}^{t} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we notice that $9$ is a power of $3,$ because $9=3^2.$ So we get\n\n$$\n3^{\\color{blue}t} = 3^{\\color{blue}2}.\n$$\n\nThe bases of both exponents are equal, so the powers must also be equal. Therefore, our solution is\n\n$$\nt = {\\color{blue}2}.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that it will take the company $\\color{blue}2$ years to reach a revenue of $\\[/math]18$ million." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148795 #edb/ref 1065 8849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148795 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "35ba03ad-41cc-5f76-9618-f78f0e3c0529" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148795 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148796 #edb/ref 1064 "We often need to know the system's state at $t=0$ when using an exponential growth model.\n\nFor example, suppose that the number of users $N(t)$ of a new phone app can be modeled by the function\n\n$$\nN(t) = N_0(1.2)^t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ represents the time, in weeks, since the app launched, and $N_0$ is a constant. If there are $5\\,000$ users $5$ weeks after launch, can we determine how many users the app had when it launched?\n\nHere, $N_0$ represents the initial number of users, and this is what we're trying to find.\n\nFrom the given information, we know that $N(5) = 5\\,000,$ So let's plug this into our function:\n\n$$\n5\\,000 = N_0(1.2)^5\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we can divide both sides by $(1.2)^5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\,000}{(1.2)^{5}} & =\\frac{{N}_{0}(1.2)^{5}}{(1.2)^{5}} \\\\ \\frac{5\\,000}{(1.2)^{5}} & =\\frac{{N}_{0}\\cancel{(1.2)^{5}}}{\\cancel{(1.2)^{5}}} \\\\ \\frac{5\\,000}{(1.2)^{5}} & ={N}_{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's flip the equation around to make it easier to work with. \n\n$$\nN_0 = \\dfrac{5\\,000}{(1.2)^5}\n$$\n\nWe can now work out $N_0$ using a calculator. We find that\n\n$$\nN_0\\approx 2\\,009.\n$$\n\nWe have rounded to the nearest integer since $N_0$ refers to a number of people.\n\nTherefore, when it launched, the app had $2\\,009$ users." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148796 #edb/ref 1065 8854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148796 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ae3d753f-4a2d-55b0-9fb7-a3f746f66018" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148796 #edb/ref 1067 "Unknown Initial Values" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175906 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175906 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175906 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175906 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186498538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175906 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186498554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175906 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving Rational Equations Containing a Binomial in One Denominator" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175906 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186175733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175906 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2e09c5b9-5f31-52a5-b696-b2df1879d289" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175907 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175907 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175907 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186498505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175907 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186498588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175907 #edb/ref 1039 "Equations Containing Negative Signs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175907 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186175739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175907 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4f3e1fc3-c46b-5958-92b2-0f2dc11ff961" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175908 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176415 #edb/ref 1759