106 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176106 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176106 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176106 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving Rational Equations Containing Binomials Using Cross-Multiplication" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176106 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186176612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176106 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176106 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176106 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f441e707-bca4-5383-a476-aeddd2c81a22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176136 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176136 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176136 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176136 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176136 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176136 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176136 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176136 #edb/ref 1045 "Speed as a Unit Rate" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176136 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186176820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176136 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176136 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176136 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186176136 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "4cf8b9a9-4050-5d59-b451-2f66b157c8dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176330 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176330 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133999" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176330 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176330 #edb/ref 1071 "It takes a particular person $2$ minutes to walk $190$ meters. Find the distance this person covers in $5$ minutes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176330 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "09b93451-12f0-5b32-9bbf-d323d2f03aae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176330 #edb/ref 1074 "Speed is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed}=\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed in meters per minute, we divide the number of meters, $190,$ by the number of minutes, $2.$ This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{190}{2} & =190÷2 \\\\  & =95.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed of the person is $95$ meters per minute. \n\nTo find the distance covered in $5$ minutes, we multiply the speed by $5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{distance} & =\\text{speed}\\times \\text{time} \\\\  & =95\\times 5 \\\\  & =475.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the person can cover $475$ meters in $5$ minutes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176612 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176612 #edb/ref 1061 143532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176612 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ecfccb89-86a2-557a-b9d3-c0ad225826b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176612 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176613 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176613 #edb/ref 1061 143533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176613 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "36c668d7-ed4b-5afb-8e0b-ee04536f38df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176613 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176614 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176614 #edb/ref 1061 143537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176614 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5d7e1964-4293-5a03-90c4-211bf2cd63f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176614 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176615 #edb/ref 1061 143541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176615 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fe911541-f3cd-5e52-abb4-31aa92a738cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176615 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176820 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176820 #edb/ref 1061 36386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176820 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "136b294f-0279-574d-9755-cfc8cb54f15d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176820 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176821 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176821 #edb/ref 1061 36387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176821 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "aed82a50-e10d-539e-895f-797eb5220670" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176821 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176822 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176822 #edb/ref 1061 36388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176822 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2e52190a-967b-5eee-8781-4b1c6cdc1bf7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176822 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176823 #edb/ref 1061 36389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176823 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cb434a73-533d-5a74-927b-b8b1bf69153e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176823 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176824 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176824 #edb/ref 1061 36390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176824 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "02f9ad99-d474-59a2-b870-d4cc8af16598" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176824 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176825 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176825 #edb/ref 1061 36391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176825 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1aec8595-c434-5eab-92bb-a1d9a3d99a15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176825 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177044 #edb/ref 1064 "**Speed** is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*.\n\nWe can calculate the speed of a moving object using the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed} = \\dfrac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that a car travels $80$ miles in $2$ hours at a constant rate. What is the speed of the car? \n\nTo find the speed (in miles per hour), we divide the number of miles, $80,$ by the number of hours, $2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{80}{2} = 40.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the car is $40$ miles per hour." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177044 #edb/ref 1065 5959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177044 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e151f782-1f59-5438-92a6-e678c9041d99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177044 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177045 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's return to the example of a car traveling at ${\\color{red}{40}}$ miles per hour. \n\nIf the car travels at this speed, it covers  a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}$ miles *every hour*. Therefore, we can deduce the following:\n\n- after ${\\color{blue}{1}}$ hour, the car covers a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}\\times{\\color{blue}{1}} = 40$ miles\n\n- after ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ hours, the car covers a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}\\times{\\color{blue}{2}} = 80$ miles\n\n- after ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ hours, the car covers a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}\\times{\\color{blue}{3}} = 120$ miles\n\n- after ${\\color{blue}{4}}$ hours, the car covers a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}\\times{\\color{blue}{4}} = 160$ miles\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nThis suggests the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{distance} = \\text{speed} \\times \\text{time}\n$$\n\nWe can use this formula to calculate the distance traveled by an object moving at a constant speed.\n\nFor instance, suppose an elevator traveled for $1.5$ minutes at a constant speed of $120$ feet per minute. What distance is covered by the elevator?\n\nThe speed is $120$ feet per minute. To find the distance covered in $1.5$ minutes, we multiply the speed by $1.5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{distance} & =\\text{speed}\\times \\text{time} \\\\  & =120\\times 1.5 \\\\  & =180.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elevator covered a distance of $180$ feet." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177045 #edb/ref 1065 5960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177045 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "95f37ad7-7647-57e9-ae85-7a3dcab40f25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177045 #edb/ref 1067 "Calculating the Distance Traveled by an Object" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177046 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's recall the formula relating distance to speed and time:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed} \\times \\text{time} = \\text{distance}\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can write this equation as\n\n$$\n\\text{time} = \\text{distance} \\div \\text{speed}\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{time} = \\dfrac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{speed}}.\n$$\n\nWe can use this formula to calculate the time taken for an object moving at a particular speed to cover a certain distance. \n\nFor example, suppose a plane flew for $3\\,120$ kilometers at a constant speed of $520$ kilometers per hour. For how long was the plane in motion?\n\nTo determine the time the plane was in motion, we divide the distance, $3\\,120$ kilometers, by the speed, $520$ kilometers per hour. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{time} & =\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{speed}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\,120}{520} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\,120÷10}{520÷10} \\\\  & =\\frac{312}{52} \\\\  & =312÷52 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the plane was in motion for $6$ hours." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177046 #edb/ref 1065 5961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177046 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "76064dfb-b2e4-56a5-95f7-2a97b1fb392b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177046 #edb/ref 1067 "Calculating the Total Time an Object is in Motion" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177094 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the rational equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12}{x+3} = \\dfrac{2}{x-2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that when the equation consists of a single rational expression on each side, like the one above, then the easiest way to solve it is usually to use cross-multiplication.\n\nHowever, before we start, note that $x=-3$ and $x=2$ cannot be solutions. This is because when $x=-3,$ we get a zero in the denominator on the left-hand side, and when $x=2,$ we get a zero in the denominator on the right-hand side.\n\nWith this in mind, let's solve the equation. First, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{x+3} & =\\frac{2}{x-2} \\\\ 12\\cdot (x-2) & =(x+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 12x-24 & =2x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-24 & =2x+6 \\\\ 12x-24-2x & =2x+6-2x \\\\ 10x-24 & =6 \\\\ 10x-24+24 & =6+24 \\\\ 10x & =30 \\\\ \\frac{10x}{10} & =\\frac{30}{10} \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=3$ is different from $x=-3$ and $x=2,$ we accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177094 #edb/ref 1065 8781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177094 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "32ce7197-6c97-5ece-b45d-186993605c4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177094 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179631 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179631 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179631 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180058 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179631 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179631 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179631 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179631 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179631 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179631 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179631 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186564464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179631 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Slope of a Line Given Standard Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179631 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179631 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "48f52c24-cd5a-5701-8ec2-1d833e87a25f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179632 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179632 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179632 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179632 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179632 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the X-Intercept of a Line" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179632 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179632 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "dd8533c7-7573-5917-b347-ce72d26a7168" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179633 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179633 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179633 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179633 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179633 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Y-Intercept of a Line" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179633 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179633 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ee8d9c8a-51c2-56b4-8006-e768bb7fa128" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179876 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179876 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179876 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179876 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179876 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179876 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179876 #edb/ref 1045 "Properties of Lines Given in Standard Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179876 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186180320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179876 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179876 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179876 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "a4c8ca08-bd22-5363-926f-e23513ac4154" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180320 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180320 #edb/ref 1061 138017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180320 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "72c1429e-a7f2-51eb-b430-aeb492262ee7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180320 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180321 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180321 #edb/ref 1061 138018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180321 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fd4133a0-8060-545f-b970-74364bd3c8af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180321 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180322 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180322 #edb/ref 1061 138022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180322 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ba8eac6e-a6b0-5050-a5e7-2508d56d4806" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180322 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180323 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180323 #edb/ref 1061 138023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180323 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8393e8f1-d3a4-51f5-bea6-a2c3546128e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180323 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180324 #edb/ref 1061 138026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180324 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3a721917-d440-59e3-a3b3-441903fcc6b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180324 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180783 #edb/ref 1064 "To determine the slope of a line that is given in standard form, we need to convert the equation to slope-intercept form.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's calculate the slope of the following line:\n\n$$\n2x + y = 8\n$$\n\nTo write this equation in slope-intercept form, we solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y & =8 \\\\ 2x+y-2x & =8-2x \\\\ y & =8-2x \\\\ y & =-2x+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the slope-intercept equation, we can tell that the slope of the line is $m=-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180783 #edb/ref 1065 8697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180783 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3550ae2f-df69-52cc-b33a-14e8f2bb2d8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180783 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180784 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to find the $x$- and $y$-intercepts of the following line:\n\n$$\n2x + y = 8\n$$\n\nLet's find each of them in turn.\n\n- To find the $x$-intercept, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+(0) & =8 \\\\ 2x & =8 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & =\\frac{8}{2} \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line intersects the $x$-axis at the point $(4,0).$\n\n- To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation and solve for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)+y & =8 \\\\ y & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line intersects the $y$-axis at the point $(0,8).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180784 #edb/ref 1065 8699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180784 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c500072d-6462-54c2-8c16-3c8f68bdbbfe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180784 #edb/ref 1067 "Determining Intercepts of Lines Given in Standard Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266880 #edb/ref 1080 "$450$ meters" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266880 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266880 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "525b1435-446e-5cc8-b37a-14999